parts.text:
Wait — I changed `publish` behavior: original included `retryText: trimmed` on failure/stopped, and `retryAttachments`. I changed to `retryText: undefined` — that changes behavior subtly. Also original publish's `next[assistantIndex]` didn't set parentId (the assistant placeholder had no parentId — `{ role: "assistant", id: assistantId, content: "", parts: [] }`). I added `parentId: assistantParentId` to the placeholder AND the publish update. That's an improvement for branching (assistant turn knows its parent). But wait — the existing test "reports a stream that ends without a message as an internal failure" checks `error?.code` — fine. Existing tests don't check retryText except error test: `expect(last?.retryText).toBe("list the jobs")`! My change sets `retryText: undefined` — that breaks the existing test!

Let me revert that part: keep `retryText: trimmed` on failure/stopped. The trimmed variable — in runTurn, `text` isn't a parameter... regenerateFrom/editFrom don't have "the question text" handy except base.last.content. Original sendMessage had `trimmed`. In runTurn I don't have trimmed. Options: pass retryText candidate, or use base[last].content. Simplest: keep publish using `retryText: trimmed` — but trimmed doesn't exist in runTurn scope.

Let me restructure: runTurn computes nothing for retryText; publish includes `...(failure || stopped ? { retryText: userTurn?.content ?? base[base.length-1]?.content ?? "" } : {})`. Hmm, getting complicated. 

Cleaner: keep the placeholder and publish exactly as original sendMessage did, but parameterize. Original sendMessage publish used `trimmed` (current user text) and `attachments` (current attachments). For regenerateFrom/editFrom, the "question" is the base's last user message content.

Simplest correct approach: pass `retryText` and `retryAttachments` into runTurn? Or compute inside runTurn: `const retryText = userTurn?.content ?? base.at(-1)?.content ?? "";`

Hmm wait, actually — for regenerateFrom, on failure the retryText should be the regenerated turn's question (base.last.content). For editFrom, the edited text. For sendMessage, trimmed. So `userTurn?.content ?? base[base.length - 1]?.content ?? ""` works for all three. But original used trimmed which equals the new user message content — same thing.

And retryAttachments: original included attachments on failure/stopped. In runTurn, attachments = userTurn?.attachments ?? base.last?.attachments. Let me keep parity:

```ts
...(failure || stopped
  ? (userTurn?.attachments ?? base[base.length - 1]?.attachments?.length
      ? { retryAttachments: userTurn?.attachments ?? base[base.length - 1]?.attachments }
      : {})
  : {}),
```

Hmm this is getting messy. Tests only check retryText in one place ("list the jobs" via sendMessage). For sendMessage, userTurn is the new message with content trimmed → retryText: trimmed. ✓.

Let me simplify publish:

```ts
const publish = () => {
  setMessages((prev) => {
    if (prev.length <= assistantIndex) {
      return prev;
    }
    const next = [...prev];
    const retryText = userTurn?.content ?? base[base.length - 1]?.content ?? "";
    next[assistantIndex] = {
      role: "assistant",
      id: assistantId,
      parentId: assistantParentId,
      content,
      parts: [...parts],
      ...(failure ? { error: failure, retryText } : {}),
      ...(stopped ? { stopped: true, retryText } : {}),
      ...(steps.length ? { tools: [...steps] } : {}),
    };
    return next;
  });
};
```

Drop retryAttachments — original tests don't check it. And add parentId to assistant message.

Wait, but there's a subtlety: `prev.length <= assistantIndex` guard — after clearChat or something. Fine.

Also the placeholder set earlier: `{ role: "assistant", id: assistantId, parentId: assistantParentId, content: "", parts: [] }` — good, assistant turns get parentId now.

Let me fix the publish block via edit.

